findings.body:
The diff changes the master-side write in `hincrbyfloatCommand` from `HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL` to `HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE`. The PR only needed to change the replicated command. Without `HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL`, `hashTypeSet` clears the field's expiry on the master: the listpack path calls `listpackExUpdateExpiry(..., HASH_LP_NO_TTL)` and the hashtable path calls `hfieldPersist`. So `HINCRBYFLOAT` on a field with a TTL now removes that TTL on the master.

This breaks the existing test `HINCRBYFLOAT - preserve expiration time of the field` (tests/unit/type/hash-field-expire.tcl:851-857), which expects the TTL to stay in the 1–20 range. It also recreates the divergence this PR is meant to fix: the master drops the TTL while the replica, which receives `HSETEX ... KEEPTTL`, keeps it.

::diff{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2556-2579"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The fix is to restore the flag on the master write. The replication rewrite does not need the master write to change.

::suggestion{file="src/t_hash.c" lines="2559"}
    hashTypeSet(c->db, o,c->argv[2]->ptr,new,HASH_SET_TAKE_VALUE | HASH_SET_KEEP_TTL);

The new test runs `r -1 hincrbyfloat h1 f1 1.1` on the master and then asserts only on the replica: `assert_range [r httl h1 FIELDS 1 f1] 90 100` (replica). It never checks `r -1 httl h1 FIELDS 1 f1`. Because the replica keeps its TTL either way, the assertion would still pass even though the master has lost the TTL, which is the exact regression in the previous finding. The test can't fail in the scenario it's meant to guard against.

Add an assertion on the master side that the field's TTL is still in range after the increment, and make sure it is set before the replica-side checks.

::code{file="tests/unit/type/hash-field-expire.tcl" lines="1990-1997"}

::suggestion{file="tests/unit/type/hash-field-expire.tcl" lines="1996"}
                assert_range [r -1 httl h1 FIELDS 1 f1] 90 100
                assert_range [r httl h1 FIELDS 1 f1] 90 100
